{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cube.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cube.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cube cube","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cube.md","fileName":"Cube.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cube","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Exponents and Roots]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Exponents and Roots","Cube Root","Sum of Cubes Identity","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cube.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cube.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cube cube","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cube.md","fileName":"Cube.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cube","heading":"Cube","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The cube of a quantity is its third power:\n\n$$a^3=a\\cdot a\\cdot a.$$\n\nThe name corresponds to the volume of a geometric cube with edge length (a). Unlike a square, a cube preserves the sign of a real number because it contains an odd number of factors: ((-a)^3=-a^3). Reversing the operation uses a [[Cube Root]]. Cubes also form reusable expansion and factorization patterns such as the [[Sum of Cubes Identity]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Exponents and Roots","Cube Root","Sum of Cubes Identity","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cube.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cube.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cube cube","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cube.md","fileName":"Cube.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cube","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[algebraessentials.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Exponents and Roots","Cube Root","Sum of Cubes Identity","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}